<mxfile host="Electron" agent="Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) draw.io/28.2.8 Chrome/140.0.7339.240 Electron/38.4.0 Safari/537.36" version="28.2.8" pages="19">
  <diagram name="Introduction" id="OcMc1TD89lyCnE7k4u9A">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1242" dy="828" grid="1" gridSize="5" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="850" pageHeight="1100" background="#CCCCCC" math="0" shadow="0" adaptiveColors="none">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="PzqoupTtZIXLCT9qukJP-0" />
        <mxCell id="PzqoupTtZIXLCT9qukJP-1" parent="PzqoupTtZIXLCT9qukJP-0" />
        <mxCell id="NfEoopE2q20AlTXDlQDK-0" value="" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=11;fontColor=default;labelBackgroundColor=default;strokeColor=default;fillColor=none;movable=1;resizable=1;rotatable=1;deletable=1;editable=1;locked=0;connectable=1;" vertex="1" parent="PzqoupTtZIXLCT9qukJP-1">
          <mxGeometry x="87" width="676.15" height="875" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="OqC8i-drENe9sEOBw3kS-1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 14px;&quot;&gt;This project begins with a simple question: &lt;b style=&quot;&quot;&gt;How can we distinguish between truth and falsehood?&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;In a world saturated with belief, bias, and speculation, the pursuit of truth often gets buried beneath inherited assumptions and emotional comfort. I wanted to peel all that away - to start from the ground up, beginning only with what we directly experience and know to be real, and then reason forward using the same logic that governs every other reliable field of inquiry.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;The purpose of this work is not to defend a side but to identify the truth, and to remove the barriers that make truth inaccessible. Many discussions about God, scripture, or existence tend to collapse into two extremes - faith without verification or skepticism without coherence - and both fall short because they bypass and abandon reasoning when it becomes uncomfortable. The aim here is to expose the bridge between belief and reason - that faith, reason, and observation necessarily coexist - showing that truth can be mapped, step by step, through consistent inference.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;This project is built on hierarchical logic - a structure where each idea depends on something more fundamental beneath it. Every section begins from observable reality and ascends layer by layer toward higher-level claims. Each claim is evaluated by the same standard: does it remain consistent with what we already know to be true? And does it explain more with fewer assumptions than its alternatives?&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;In other words:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;-&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;If two claims conflict, then only one can align fully with reality.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;-&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;If a claim cannot be tested by the same principles that test every other truth, then it cannot be reliably known.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;pointerEvents=0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;gradientColor=#b3b3b3;strokeColor=#666666;spacingTop=4;spacingLeft=6;spacingRight=6;" vertex="1" parent="PzqoupTtZIXLCT9qukJP-1">
          <mxGeometry x="100" y="75" width="650" height="290" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <UserObject label="&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 17px;&quot;&gt;Introduction &amp;amp; Background&lt;/font&gt;" link="data:page/id,lkuSvPZcJC7dHRsydWrw" id="gpQ3GL357HhQMA6Q2Nzm-0">
          <mxCell style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fontStyle=1;fillColor=#E6E6E6;fontSize=18;" vertex="1" parent="PzqoupTtZIXLCT9qukJP-1">
            <mxGeometry x="100" y="15" width="650" height="45" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="YUl1blt-yoo7c7PEC4xC-0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent;&quot;&gt;&lt;b style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 16px;&quot;&gt;Structure and Method&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;The project is built as a logical map rather than a linear argument. Each header functions as a node - a concise statement of conclusion at that level - while the text beneath it provides the reasoning and evidence that led there. Every argument and worldview is first steelmanned - presented in its strongest, most coherent form before critique - so that no belief is dismissed prematurely or unfairly. This ensures that each claim - whether theistic or skeptical - is evaluated on its best possible grounds, and then compared directly against reality.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;- A reader who only wants the overview can read the headers alone and still understand the logical skeleton of the argument.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;-&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;A read&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;&quot;&gt;er who wants to verify can open a section and trace how each conclusion was derived.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;A&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;reader who disagrees can immediately see where and why - since every conclusion explicitly depends on what precedes it.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;This modular hierarchy allows the work to scale without collapsing into narrative confusion. It also removes the intimidation of dense academic writing by revealing the structure of thought itself - where disagreement can be explicitly and precisely pinpointed rather than argued abstractly.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;pointerEvents=0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;gradientColor=#b3b3b3;strokeColor=#666666;spacingTop=6;spacingLeft=6;spacingRight=6;" vertex="1" parent="PzqoupTtZIXLCT9qukJP-1">
          <mxGeometry x="100" y="380" width="650" height="230" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="AryiAfD0HVLzF7PLgP97-0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: transparent;&quot;&gt;&lt;b style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 16px;&quot;&gt;Why this Approach Matters&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;The modern world often treats &quot;truth&quot; as fundamentally subjective, yet every field that actually works - science, language, mathematics - depends on the opposite: that contradictions cannot both be true, and that claims must correspond to reality. In questions of belief, this standard is often abandoned in favor of comfort, speculation, identity, or habit. But comfort is not evidence, and familiarity is not proof.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;This project restores clarity to belief by exposing its structure. Every conviction - religious, secular, or otherwise - rests on something, and that foundation can be traced, examined, and understood. By making reasoning hierarchical and transparent, the framework lets anyone navigate and see exactly what their worldview stands on: where it is grounded in fact, where it relies on inference, and where it rests on faith.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 11px;&quot;&gt;Its goal is not to argue for or against belief, but to make belief visible - to demistify apparent complexity - turning confusion into awareness. In doing so, it offers a common ground for honest thought: a way for anyone, from any background, to see truth and falsehood for what they are, rather than what they feel.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;pointerEvents=0;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontSize=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;gradientColor=#b3b3b3;strokeColor=#666666;spacingTop=6;spacingLeft=6;spacingRight=6;" vertex="1" parent="PzqoupTtZIXLCT9qukJP-1">
          <mxGeometry x="100.07" y="625" width="650" height="230" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
